<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Processor Performance — {{companyName}}</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="assets/base.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="assets/reports.css">
<style>
  /* Print fit — template-local, like the challan copymark. The report layer is
     screen-first (1280px); the shared renderer prints fixed A4 portrait, so
     scale the sheet to the printable width instead of clipping columns. */
  @media print{ .report{zoom:.8;} }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="report">

  <div class="report-head">
    <h1>Processor Performance<span>Loss against tolerance &middot; {{windowLabel}}</span></h1>
    <div class="report-meta">
      <strong>{{upper companyName}}</strong>
      GSTIN {{companyGstin}}<br>
      {{periodLabel}}
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="kpis">
    <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Processors</div><div class="value">{{kpiProcessors}}</div><div class="sub">across {{processTypeCount}} process type{{#unless processTypeCountIsOne}}s{{/unless}}</div></div>
    <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Orders Closed</div><div class="value">{{kpiOrdersClosed}}</div><div class="sub">in the window</div></div>
    <div class="kpi is-critical"><div class="label">Over Tolerance</div><div class="value">{{kpiOverTolerance}}</div><div class="sub">processors</div></div>
    <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Recovered</div><div class="value">&#8377;{{kpiRecovered}}</div><div class="sub">via debit notes</div></div>
  </div>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Processor</th><th style="width:82px">Process</th>
        <th class="num" style="width:52px">Orders</th><th class="num" style="width:78px">Issued</th>
        <th class="num" style="width:56px">Tol. %</th><th class="num" style="width:62px">Actual %</th>
        <th style="width:120px">Against tolerance</th>
        <th class="num" style="width:70px">Debited ₹</th><th style="width:82px">Status</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {{#each rows}}
      <tr{{#if this.rowClass}} class="{{this.rowClass}}"{{/if}}>
        <td>{{this.processorName}}</td><td>{{this.processType}}</td><td class="num">{{this.ordersCompleted}}</td><td class="num">{{this.issued}}</td>
        <td class="num">{{this.tolerance}}</td><td class="num">{{this.actualLoss}}</td>
        <td><div class="bar"><i{{#if this.barOver}} class="over"{{/if}} style="width:{{this.barWidthPct}}%"></i><u style="left:{{this.tickLeftPct}}%"></u></div></td>
        <td class="num">{{this.debited}}</td><td><span class="chip {{this.chipClass}}">{{this.chipLabel}}</span></td>
      </tr>
      {{/each}}
      <tr class="grp"><td colspan="7">{{kpiProcessors}} processor{{#unless kpiProcessorsIsOne}}s{{/unless}} &middot; {{kpiOrdersClosed}} order{{#unless kpiOrdersClosedIsOne}}s{{/unless}} closed</td><td class="num">{{totalDebited}}</td><td></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <b>What this report is actually for</b>
    Per-processor tolerance is only meaningful if you have evidence to set it with. Without this report you end up
    giving a reliable dyer and a cheap one the same 3%, which quietly subsidises the cheap one. Loss inside tolerance
    never appears anywhere: it is absorbed into finished goods cost by design &mdash; the debit-note column shows only
    the portion that was caught.
  </div>

  <div class="report-foot">
    <span>Tick on each bar marks the agreed tolerance &middot; bars turn terracotta only when actual exceeds it</span>
    <span>{{companyName}} &middot; generated {{generatedOn}}</span>
  </div>
</div>
</body>
</html>
